{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790336814,"nonce":"o9nWXjbH7BekBiCjqICHXf6eOLg=","title":"A `break` or `continue` outside every loop lowers to nothing","message":"`SsaBuilder::process_statement` takes a `break` or `continue` that lies in no loop (the CFG split leaves it as a statement when its loop stack is empty) as a statement of no effect: control falls through to what follows. Reached, that is a silent miscompile; it should be refused at lowering, or at least trap.\n\nIt cannot be refused yet because the Python frontend emits such statements on paths that never run: after a `try`, `lower.rs` emits `if flag == 2 { break }` and `if flag == 3 { continue }` whenever the body was redirected, whether or not a loop encloses the `try` (see the `for code in 1..=3` loop at the end of the try lowering). Refusing them fails conformance cases exceptions/handler_done_with_exception, scope/nonlocal_write_only, inference/none_after_assignment, iteration/for_else, iteration/while_else, modules/json_and_globals.\n\nFix: have the Python lowering emit the break/continue dispatch only when a loop encloses the `try` (loop_elses / loop_depth know), check the other frontends do the same, then make the compiler refuse a `break`/`continue` with no loop.","files":null}]}